The mass of the water is [X] (Note: The specific heat capacity of water is 4.2×10³J/(kg·℃);) The calorific value of ethanol is 3.0×10^7 J/kg. If we already know the answer to the above question is 8.4×10^6 J, then what should the content corresponding to [X] in the question be?

solution:
The heat absorbed by the water is calculated using $ Q = mc\Delta T $. Given $ Q = 8.4 \times 10^6 \, \text{J} $, $ c = 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $, and $ \Delta T = 40 \, \text{°C} $, solving for mass $ m $:  
$$
m = \frac{Q}{c\Delta T} = \frac{8.4 \times 10^6}{4.2 \times 10^3 \times 40} = \frac{8.4 \times 10^6}{1.68 \times 10^5} = 50 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{50 kg}

The water heater's capacity is 100L, equivalent to 100 kg of water. The electrical energy provided is 55% of the total heat required. Electrical energy is $ P \cdot t = 1650 \, \text{W} \cdot 4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{s} = 6.93 \times 10^6 \, \text{J} $. Total heat $ Q = \frac{6.93 \times 10^6}{0.55} = 1.26 \times 10^7 \, \text{J} $. Using $ Q = mc\Delta T $, solve for $ \Delta T $:  
$$
\Delta T = \frac{1.26 \times 10^7}{100 \cdot 4.2 \times 10^3} = 30^\circ\text{C}
$$  
Thus, $ X = 20^\circ\text{C} + 30^\circ\text{C} = 50^\circ\text{C} $.  

Final answer: \boxed{50^\circ\text{C}}

The heat absorbed by water is calculated using $ Q = mc\Delta T $. Rearranging for $ \Delta T $:  
$$
\Delta T = \frac{Q}{mc} = \frac{8.4 \times 10^3}{0.2 \times 4.2 \times 10^3} = \frac{8.4}{0.84} = 10\,^\circ\text{C}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{10\,^\circ\text{C}}

The heat absorbed by the copper is calculated using $ Q = mc\Delta T $. Given $ Q = 14742 \, \text{J} $, $ c = 0.39 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} $, and $ \Delta T = 1070 - 20 = 1050 \, \text{°C} $, solving for mass $ X $:  
$$
X = \frac{Q}{c \Delta T} = \frac{14742}{0.39 \times 10^3 \times 1050} = \frac{14742}{409500} \approx 0.036 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{0.036 kg}

The heat absorbed by the water is given by $ Q = mc\Delta T $. Rearranging for mass $ m $:  
$$
m = \frac{Q}{c\Delta T} = \frac{2.52 \times 10^6 \, \text{J}}{4.2 \times 10^3 \, \text{J/(kg·°C)} \times 60 \, \text{°C}} = \frac{2.52 \times 10^6}{2.52 \times 10^5} = 10 \, \text{kg}
$$  
Final answer: \boxed{10 kg}

